{"name":"Hunter eyes","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Hunter-eyes_-_Delapouite_-_game-icons.svg/960px-Hunter-eyes_-_Delapouite_-_game-icons.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hunter-eyes_-_Delapouite_-_game-icons.svg","caption":"Hunter-eyes - Delapouite - game-icons","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":76,"casual":{"lead":["Hunter eyes describes an eye area that looks intense and masculine: deep-set, slightly hooded, with a positive canthal tilt, little visible white below the iris and low-set brows. It is contrasted with so-called prey eyes that are rounder, show more white and tilt downward.","Users attribute the look to bone structure such as orbit shape, brow ridge and cheekbone support, plus soft-tissue factors like lid hooding. The term has no formal definition in ophthalmology or anthropology, and no study has tested it as a category."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["People approximate the look with grooming and makeup, such as brow shaping, eyeliner and lash styling, and by lowering body fat to show the orbital area. Some try squinting or exercises, though users disagree on whether those change anything; surgical options discussed include canthoplasty, upper blepharoplasty and orbital procedures."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["The concept itself carries no direct risk. Surgical and filler procedures talked about for the look carry standard risks such as asymmetry, dry eye, vision-related complications and revision surgery. Some critics link the trend to appearance anxiety."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Hunter-eyes_-_Delapouite_-_game-icons.svg/960px-Hunter-eyes_-_Delapouite_-_game-icons.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.o